WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.260
Welcome to another Stories From The
Stores

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.200
I'm joined today by curator Alex Rose

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:12.660
and she's telling us about Britain's
obsession with the weather or at least clouds

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.427
What's going on Alex?

00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:17.020
People have long been fascinated with the clouds

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:20.570
but clouds have long been a problem for
scientists because they're so transient.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.900
By the time you've described one it's
kind of changed into something else.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.200
So they always felt like they were
beyond scientific description.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.700
I mean is there really any scientific
description needed, because you've got three:

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.560
big, small, dinosaur!

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.860
Yeah that pretty much sums it up

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.560
but there was a man working in the early 19th
century who wanted to give more

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.670
scientific names to the clouds and so he
came up with a classification system

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.320
that divided them into three main
families. He gave them Latin names though.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.880
He called them Cirrus which is
the Latin word for curl of hair

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.800
Stratus which means layer

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.660
and Cumulus which means heap or pile.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.140
In order to capture the sky that he was looking at and help his own understanding

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.620
he made lots and lots of sketches and watercolors of the sky and the cloud structures

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.000
and that was what helped him to see patterns in
what he was looking at and to come up

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.200
with his classification system.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.220
So are these sort of specific forms of clouds here?

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.830
Yeah that's right, so here we've mostly got a cumulus cloud

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.300
so the heap or pile, and there's also some
Cirrus and Stratus up in the sky.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.500
This one is a kind of hybrid version. Howard also wanted to describe how clouds changed

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.460
how they join together and split
apart, so this is a Cumulostratus cloud.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.420
So you say it's science but this
looks more like art, why did he draw these?

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.020
It was to help him understand his own observations.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.100
Because the clouds kept changing so he needed to
find a way of recording them and

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.380
comparing things he'd seen from day to
day.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.420
On these he worked with another artist who filled in the landscapes

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.440
but art works like these are essential for

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.160
him communicating his ideas to the wider
scientific world.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.200
Okay so he's classified the clouds from
these drawings but why do we care?

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:11.780
It's a good question.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.000
So when Howard was working, meteorology was still a really young science

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.240
and it was still very much about gathering as much data as you possibly could

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.260
in as many observations as you could

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.940
and kind of hoping that patterns would appear from those observations

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:30.280
But Howard's scheme allowed us to start
to view the clouds in a scientific way.

